<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        A staff identity number becomes optional, and stays unique when it is there.

        WHY IT WAS WRONG TO REQUIRE IT
        This table exists so that a hospital can hold a file on everyone who works in it, including the people who have
        no system account: a cleaner, a porter, a driver. The record's own documentation says so. Requiring a national
        identity number at the same time makes that impossible for anyone who does not have one to hand, which is the
        one thing the entity was written to allow.

        WHY UNIQUE STAYS
        The number is what the record is keyed on, and names repeat, are spelled several ways and change. Two staff
        files sharing a number would be one person on file twice.

        WHY NOTHING BREAKS
        Postgres permits any number of NULLs inside a unique index, so "unique when present" is exactly what the
        constraint already enforces once the NOT NULL is dropped. No index change, no partial index needed.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20261005130000-1" author="hms">
        <comment>Make StaffRecord.national_id optional while keeping it unique when present</comment>
        <dropNotNullConstraint tableName="staff_record" columnName="national_id" columnDataType="varchar(32)"/>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
